// TEST: Caret Fold Kernel Is Not Vacuous (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: the caret is a rank CLAIM, not a shorthand
// ============================================================================
// A `T^k` parameter must MEAN rank k at every seam, including the one where
// the annotated name never reaches a demand site.
//
// `reduce(A, f)` hands its kernel the ELEMENT type, so a `T^1` fold kernel
// over a scalar-element array is a rank contradiction and is refused -- the
// verdict the LAMBDA spelling of the same kernel already gave.
//
// The NAMED spelling below used to be accepted instead, and silently folded
// SCALARS: over a rank-2 operand `reduce(g, addrow)` returned the innermost
// (row) sums [3, 33, 63, 93], with the `T^1` on both parameters contributing
// nothing. One kernel body, two meanings by spelling.
//
// The loss was in `unify`, not in `reduce`. An arity pin lives on an inference
// var ID (`Subst.arityConstraints`), and a var-to-var bind moved the pinned var
// behind an UNPINNED survivor without carrying the pin over -- the survivor
// then answered `GetArityConstraint = None` and every later check read "no
// claim". The seam that supplies the unpinned survivor is an UNANNOTATED
// RETURN TYPE: `= a + b` unifies the signature's `T^1` var against the decl's
// fresh retType var. Spelling the identical function `-> T^1` made retType the
// SAME var, so no bind happened, so nothing was lost -- which is why the two
// spellings below disagreed. `CopyPolymorphic`, the rank lower bound, and
// `CopyLiteralDefault` all already travel on that bind; the arity pin now does
// too.
// ============================================================================
function addrow(a: T^1, b: T^1) = a + b

let g: Array<Int64 like Idx<4>, Idx<3>> = [[0, 1, 2], [10, 11, 12], [20, 21, 22], [30, 31, 32]]

let named = reduce(g, addrow)
